   "flags": [
    "isolated_O_atom_reference_outside_model_domain"
   ],
   "note": "Most stable site is fcc hollow: -4.436 eV (3x3x4), with hcp at -4.422 (+0.014 eV) and top at -2.54. Bridge is not a minimum. Numerical convergence: 2x2x4 gives -4.428 and 3x3x5 gives -4.436, so size and coverage effects are under 0.01 eV. PREMISE CAVEAT: an MLIP's isolated-atom energy (here E(O) = -2.082 eV) is a fitted atomic reference energy, not a spin-polarized triplet O atom computed in DFT, so the atom-referenced number carries a reference error that size convergence cannot remove. VALIDATION: (1) Independent known result: the model's O2 binding energy, 2E(O) - E(O2), is 5.53 eV with d(O-O) = 1.237 A. Experiment gives De of about 5.2 eV (D0 = 5.12 eV). PBE, the model's training level, typically overbinds O2 by about 1 eV, so the model's atom reference agrees with neither experiment nor its own training functional, and is uncertain by several tenths of an eV per O. (2) Literature DFT for O/Cu(111) relative to the O atom: Xu & Mavrikakis, Surf. Sci. 2001 (GGA-PW91) report a threefold-hollow binding energy of about -4.3 eV, and another DFT study reports fcc -5.00 and hcp -4.905 eV. Our -4.44 eV and the fcc preference fall inside that spread. UNCERTAINTY: +/-0.5 eV, dominated by the atomic reference and by MLIP versus DFT error. It covers the spread of the literature DFT values and the O2-binding discrepancy, and does not cover the error of DFT relative to experiment. The well-posed alternative, referenced to 1/2 O2(g) from the same model, is -1.67 eV (fcc). It cancels most of the atomic-reference error but inherits the O2 description error.",
